WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.333 --> 00:00:08.103
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.103 --> 00:00:09.883
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:09.883 --> 00:00:12.773
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.793 --> 00:00:14.453
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.453 --> 00:00:16.993
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.003 --> 00:00:18.903
things about the world at the same

00:00:18.903 --> 00:00:20.493
time as improving your English.

00:00:20.903 --> 00:00:23.543
I'm Alastair Budge, and today it's

00:00:23.543 --> 00:00:25.523
the third and final part of our

00:00:25.523 --> 00:00:28.903
mini-series on Nelson and Lady Hamilton.

00:00:29.383 --> 00:00:31.163
In part one, we followed

00:00:31.193 --> 00:00:33.873
Nelson's rise from sickly Norfolk

00:00:33.873 --> 00:00:36.273
schoolboy to hero of the Nile.

00:00:36.573 --> 00:00:39.243
In part two, Emma Hamilton clawed her

00:00:39.243 --> 00:00:41.433
way up from a blacksmith's cottage

00:00:41.673 --> 00:00:43.633
to the top of European society.

00:00:44.393 --> 00:00:47.543
Today, their two stories become one: a

00:00:47.553 --> 00:00:50.163
love affair that scandalised Britain, a

00:00:50.173 --> 00:00:53.113
battle that made one of them immortal, and

00:00:53.113 --> 00:00:56.133
a dying wish that Britain chose to ignore.

00:00:56.813 --> 00:00:58.723
OK then, let's not waste a

00:00:58.733 --> 00:01:01.123
minute and get right into it.

00:01:03.143 --> 00:01:04.743
It is the morning of the

00:01:04.783 --> 00:01:08.119
21st of October, 1805.

00:01:09.174 --> 00:01:11.133
Off the southern coast of Spain,

00:01:11.493 --> 00:01:14.313
near a cape called Trafalgar, the

00:01:14.313 --> 00:01:16.763
British fleet is preparing for battle.

00:01:18.113 --> 00:01:20.553
On board his flagship, HMS

00:01:20.563 --> 00:01:23.063
Victory, Admiral Horatio

00:01:23.063 --> 00:01:25.633
Nelson sits alone in his cabin.

00:01:27.113 --> 00:01:29.963
You will remember from part one how

00:01:29.963 --> 00:01:32.693
this day is going to end, but we must

00:01:32.693 --> 00:01:35.073
return to this morning, because Nelson

00:01:35.413 --> 00:01:37.623
has a few things to take care of.

00:01:38.763 --> 00:01:40.283
He knows the fighting

00:01:40.293 --> 00:01:41.923
will begin within hours.

00:01:42.473 --> 00:01:44.393
He knows, too, that there is a

00:01:44.413 --> 00:01:46.873
good chance he will not survive it.

00:01:47.943 --> 00:01:50.573
So he opens his private diary

00:01:50.913 --> 00:01:53.653
and writes one final request.

00:01:54.413 --> 00:01:57.283
It is a codicil, an addition to his

00:01:57.293 --> 00:02:00.533
will, and its subject is a woman.

00:02:02.213 --> 00:02:05.063
"I leave Emma Lady Hamilton," he

00:02:05.063 --> 00:02:07.313
writes, "a legacy to my King and

00:02:07.313 --> 00:02:09.743
Country, that they will give her ample

00:02:09.843 --> 00:02:12.473
provision to maintain her rank in life."

00:02:13.973 --> 00:02:16.783
He calls two of his captains, Hardy

00:02:16.783 --> 00:02:19.323
and Blackwood, into the cabin, and

00:02:19.533 --> 00:02:22.143
they sign their names as witnesses.

00:02:23.503 --> 00:02:25.933
A few hours later, he is shot.

00:02:26.813 --> 00:02:29.173
And among his last words, as he

00:02:29.173 --> 00:02:32.693
lies dying below deck, is a plea

00:02:33.043 --> 00:02:34.853
to the captain kneeling beside him:

00:02:35.983 --> 00:02:38.583
take care of my dear Lady Hamilton.

00:02:40.063 --> 00:02:42.453
The most famous man in Britain spent

00:02:42.463 --> 00:02:44.933
his last hours of peace, and then

00:02:45.103 --> 00:02:47.573
some of his last breaths, asking

00:02:47.573 --> 00:02:49.923
his country to look after a woman,

00:02:50.533 --> 00:02:52.733
a woman who was not his wife.

00:02:54.163 --> 00:02:56.503
This episode is the story of

00:02:56.503 --> 00:02:59.023
how Emma earned that dying wish,

00:02:59.183 --> 00:03:01.303
and of what Britain did with it.

00:03:02.383 --> 00:03:04.933
So, let's go back seven years,

00:03:05.043 --> 00:03:08.433
to September 1798, and to the

00:03:08.433 --> 00:03:10.893
Bay of Naples, where we left off.

00:03:11.819 --> 00:03:14.109
Nelson had just destroyed the

00:03:14.119 --> 00:03:16.429
French fleet at the Nile, and

00:03:16.519 --> 00:03:19.049
he arrived in Naples a hero.

00:03:20.449 --> 00:03:22.869
He was also physically wrecked,

00:03:23.169 --> 00:03:25.299
not just from his most recent

00:03:25.319 --> 00:03:28.239
battle, but by years of fighting.

00:03:29.199 --> 00:03:30.649
He had lost his right arm.

00:03:30.979 --> 00:03:33.579
He had lost most of the sight in one eye.

00:03:34.199 --> 00:03:36.669
He had a fresh head wound, few

00:03:36.669 --> 00:03:39.069
teeth left and a terrible cough.

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:43.959
Emma organised his hero's welcome, nursed

00:03:43.959 --> 00:03:46.329
him back to health in the embassy, and

00:03:46.339 --> 00:03:49.039
threw a party for his fortieth birthday

00:03:49.279 --> 00:03:52.089
with almost two thousand guests.

00:03:53.582 --> 00:03:56.652
When news of the victory had first reached

00:03:56.652 --> 00:03:59.942
Naples, Emma had written to Nelson to

00:03:59.942 --> 00:04:02.022
tell him that she had fainted with joy.

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:05.050
Now she made sure his name was

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:08.370
everywhere: on ribbons, on buttons,

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:10.390
on banners across the city.

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:14.140
And Nelson, it must be said,

00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:16.339
loved every minute of it.

00:04:16.819 --> 00:04:19.779
He was brave and brilliant, but he

00:04:19.779 --> 00:04:22.239
was hopelessly vulnerable to praise.

00:04:22.929 --> 00:04:24.009
Everyone knew it.

00:04:24.629 --> 00:04:25.789
Emma certainly did.

00:04:26.859 --> 00:04:28.969
One woman who later watched the

00:04:28.969 --> 00:04:30.229
two of them together at dinner

00:04:30.739 --> 00:04:34.019
wrote that Emma blew flattery full

00:04:34.039 --> 00:04:36.649
in his face, and that he happily

00:04:36.659 --> 00:04:38.949
breathed in every last bit of it.

00:04:40.289 --> 00:04:42.679
And within a few months, Emma

00:04:42.679 --> 00:04:44.199
would show that she was much

00:04:44.229 --> 00:04:46.129
more than a glamorous hostess.

00:04:47.289 --> 00:04:50.359
In December 1798, the French

00:04:50.389 --> 00:04:53.119
army was advancing on Naples, and

00:04:53.329 --> 00:04:55.499
the royal family had to escape.

00:04:56.359 --> 00:04:58.719
They were loaded onto Nelson's ships at

00:04:58.719 --> 00:05:00.879
night, along with their treasure, and

00:05:01.139 --> 00:05:04.209
sailed for Palermo, in Sicily, straight

00:05:04.349 --> 00:05:06.939
into one of the worst storms Nelson

00:05:06.939 --> 00:05:08.979
said he had ever experienced at sea.

00:05:10.339 --> 00:05:13.259
Almost everyone on board was helpless with

00:05:13.259 --> 00:05:15.479
seasickness, including the King and Queen.

00:05:16.359 --> 00:05:18.449
Emma spent the crossing on her

00:05:18.459 --> 00:05:20.619
feet, nursing the royal children.

00:05:21.579 --> 00:05:23.959
And when the Queen's six-year-old

00:05:23.959 --> 00:05:27.509
son, Prince Alberto, sadly fell into

00:05:27.509 --> 00:05:30.469
convulsions, it was Emma who held him.

00:05:31.569 --> 00:05:34.169
He died in her arms on Christmas Day.

00:05:35.439 --> 00:05:37.959
For the rest of her life, Emma kept

00:05:37.959 --> 00:05:40.349
a small brooch containing a lock of

00:05:40.349 --> 00:05:42.939
his hair, engraved with the words

00:05:43.369 --> 00:05:45.629
"Prince Albert died in my arms".

00:05:46.849 --> 00:05:49.409
Emma and Maria Carolina were already

00:05:49.409 --> 00:05:51.699
close, but by the time they had

00:05:51.699 --> 00:05:54.099
arrived in Palermo, the blacksmith's

00:05:54.099 --> 00:05:56.059
daughter had become something like

00:05:56.079 --> 00:05:58.159
family to the Queen of Naples.

00:05:59.309 --> 00:06:02.169
And she had also grown a lot

00:06:02.229 --> 00:06:04.609
closer to the British admiral.

00:06:05.729 --> 00:06:08.799
Now, Nelson had a devoted wife

00:06:08.799 --> 00:06:10.569
at home in England: Fanny.

00:06:11.779 --> 00:06:14.049
While her husband had been spending his

00:06:14.049 --> 00:06:16.809
evenings at balls and card tables with

00:06:16.839 --> 00:06:19.979
Emma and the royal court, Fanny was

00:06:19.979 --> 00:06:22.369
living quietly in the English countryside.

00:06:23.199 --> 00:06:25.509
She spent much of her time caring for

00:06:25.509 --> 00:06:28.149
Nelson's elderly father, a Norfolk

00:06:28.159 --> 00:06:30.729
clergyman, and writing letters to her

00:06:30.739 --> 00:06:33.989
husband that increasingly went unanswered.

00:06:35.159 --> 00:06:38.269
Emma, for her part, had Sir William,

00:06:38.579 --> 00:06:40.449
her husband and the leading British

00:06:40.449 --> 00:06:42.439
diplomat to the Kingdom of Naples.

00:06:44.189 --> 00:06:45.809
It didn't seem to make a difference.

00:06:46.679 --> 00:06:50.029
Nelson and Emma fell completely in

00:06:50.039 --> 00:06:53.199
love and embarked on a very public

00:06:53.229 --> 00:06:55.509
affair, with the full blessing

00:06:55.789 --> 00:06:57.849
of Emma's husband, Sir William.

00:06:59.005 --> 00:07:01.005
It was a strange business.

00:07:01.575 --> 00:07:03.165
Modern, ahead of its

00:07:03.165 --> 00:07:04.175
time, you might think.

00:07:05.085 --> 00:07:06.585
In the late eighteenth century,

00:07:06.855 --> 00:07:08.745
it was certainly scandalous.

00:07:10.045 --> 00:07:11.945
What happened was that Nelson,

00:07:12.545 --> 00:07:15.525
Emma and Sir William began living

00:07:15.525 --> 00:07:18.295
together, openly, as a trio.

00:07:19.275 --> 00:07:21.625
They even gave themselves a nickname, a

00:07:21.625 --> 00:07:24.945
Latin motto meaning "three joined in one".

00:07:26.255 --> 00:07:28.135
Guests at their dinners would

00:07:28.135 --> 00:07:30.005
watch Emma sing songs in praise

00:07:30.005 --> 00:07:31.765
of Nelson's victories while her

00:07:31.765 --> 00:07:34.025
husband nodded along approvingly.

00:07:34.885 --> 00:07:37.975
Sir William, who was by now nearly

00:07:37.985 --> 00:07:40.545
seventy years old, seems to have accepted

00:07:40.545 --> 00:07:43.105
the situation with remarkable calm.

00:07:44.168 --> 00:07:46.583
Perhaps this was partly because the

00:07:46.583 --> 00:07:49.043
two men were devoted to each other.

00:07:49.833 --> 00:07:52.393
Nelson had long looked up to Sir

00:07:52.413 --> 00:07:55.043
William, this cultured, worldly

00:07:55.043 --> 00:07:57.333
man almost thirty years his senior.

00:07:58.153 --> 00:07:59.933
And Sir William, who had no

00:07:59.933 --> 00:08:01.903
children of his own, treated the

00:08:01.913 --> 00:08:04.343
great admiral almost like a son.

00:08:05.323 --> 00:08:07.573
He adored Emma, she adored him,

00:08:08.193 --> 00:08:10.273
and this arrangement seemed

00:08:10.333 --> 00:08:12.113
to suit everyone perfectly.

00:08:13.093 --> 00:08:15.023
Well, everyone in Sicily.

00:08:15.943 --> 00:08:18.793
In London, Nelson's superiors were

00:08:18.823 --> 00:08:21.673
increasingly frustrated: the hero

00:08:21.703 --> 00:08:24.383
of the Nile was idling at a foreign

00:08:24.383 --> 00:08:27.533
court, seemingly unable to tear himself

00:08:27.533 --> 00:08:29.573
away from the ambassador's wife.

00:08:30.544 --> 00:08:32.024
There was a particular

00:08:32.024 --> 00:08:34.034
flavour to the disapproval.

00:08:34.964 --> 00:08:37.804
To respectable English eyes, the

00:08:37.804 --> 00:08:39.944
courts of southern Europe were places

00:08:39.944 --> 00:08:43.744
of luxury, idleness and loose morals,

00:08:43.954 --> 00:08:46.524
places where a good, plain English

00:08:46.534 --> 00:08:49.214
hero could be softened and corrupted.

00:08:50.294 --> 00:08:53.264
People reached for an ancient comparison:

00:08:53.624 --> 00:08:55.914
Antony and Cleopatra, the great Roman

00:08:55.914 --> 00:08:58.834
general ruined by his foreign temptress.

00:08:59.987 --> 00:09:02.517
Although Emma was English, she

00:09:02.527 --> 00:09:04.807
was the foreign temptress, leading

00:09:04.967 --> 00:09:06.967
the British naval hero astray.

00:09:08.091 --> 00:09:10.191
He ignored more than one order

00:09:10.231 --> 00:09:12.901
to move on, and in 1800 he

00:09:12.901 --> 00:09:15.271
was, in effect, called home.

00:09:16.467 --> 00:09:18.637
The British press was no kinder.

00:09:19.237 --> 00:09:21.547
When the three of them finally

00:09:21.547 --> 00:09:23.917
returned to England, the newspapers

00:09:24.127 --> 00:09:26.787
and the cartoonists feasted on them.

00:09:27.487 --> 00:09:30.187
And underneath the mockery,

00:09:30.347 --> 00:09:32.007
there was class tension.

00:09:32.787 --> 00:09:34.847
The moment Emma opened her mouth in

00:09:34.847 --> 00:09:37.707
a London drawing room, everyone could

00:09:37.707 --> 00:09:39.787
hear exactly where she came from.

00:09:40.357 --> 00:09:43.997
In Naples her accent had been irrelevant;

00:09:44.277 --> 00:09:46.977
in England, it placed her instantly,

00:09:47.277 --> 00:09:49.717
and permanently, as an outsider.

00:09:50.837 --> 00:09:52.467
Nelson, for that matter,

00:09:52.777 --> 00:09:54.687
was not high society either.

00:09:55.217 --> 00:09:57.577
He was a country parson's son who

00:09:57.577 --> 00:10:00.317
had risen on talent and courage, and

00:10:00.597 --> 00:10:02.987
the aristocrats who ran Britain never

00:10:03.207 --> 00:10:05.967
entirely let either of them forget it.

00:10:06.647 --> 00:10:08.917
In their eyes, the affair was two

00:10:08.967 --> 00:10:11.737
upstarts making a spectacle of themselves.

00:10:12.727 --> 00:10:15.707
Nelson's loyal wife, Fanny, was

00:10:15.717 --> 00:10:18.427
humiliated, and Nelson treated her

00:10:18.477 --> 00:10:21.507
terribly, simply ignoring her and

00:10:21.507 --> 00:10:23.507
then separating from her entirely.

00:10:24.807 --> 00:10:28.547
And in January 1801, in secret,

00:10:28.867 --> 00:10:31.127
Emma gave birth to a daughter.

00:10:31.787 --> 00:10:34.107
The little girl was named Horatia,

00:10:34.377 --> 00:10:36.347
and the baby was smuggled out of

00:10:36.357 --> 00:10:38.567
the house to a nurse within days.

00:10:39.733 --> 00:10:42.503
Officially, for years, nobody would

00:10:42.503 --> 00:10:45.613
admit whose child she was, although it

00:10:45.613 --> 00:10:47.753
didn't take much ingenuity to guess.

00:10:48.973 --> 00:10:51.473
Nelson bought a house in the countryside

00:10:51.503 --> 00:10:53.873
outside London, a place called Merton,

00:10:54.103 --> 00:10:56.443
and Emma threw herself into making

00:10:56.443 --> 00:10:58.783
it a home for the three of them, a

00:10:58.823 --> 00:11:01.543
kind of shrine to Nelson's victories.

00:11:02.563 --> 00:11:05.463
The house, the entertaining, the constant

00:11:05.463 --> 00:11:08.443
stream of guests: all of this cost money.

00:11:08.893 --> 00:11:12.353
And money was about to become a problem.

00:11:13.543 --> 00:11:16.663
For all his fame, remember, Nelson

00:11:16.673 --> 00:11:19.553
was not a rich man, not compared to

00:11:19.553 --> 00:11:21.543
the aristocrats of the day at least.

00:11:22.013 --> 00:11:24.533
He was a vicar's son living on his

00:11:24.533 --> 00:11:27.423
pay and his prize money, and he had

00:11:27.423 --> 00:11:29.893
to borrow money just to buy Merton.

00:11:31.323 --> 00:11:34.213
Emma, meanwhile, spent the way she

00:11:34.213 --> 00:11:36.283
had learned to spend in the palaces

00:11:36.283 --> 00:11:39.553
of Naples: as if money were no object.

00:11:40.613 --> 00:11:42.753
Sir William contributed to this

00:11:42.763 --> 00:11:45.293
lifestyle — after all, Emma was still

00:11:45.293 --> 00:11:47.783
his wife — but there were constant

00:11:47.783 --> 00:11:49.643
question marks about how things

00:11:49.643 --> 00:11:52.353
would be paid for, and how long this

00:11:52.383 --> 00:11:54.763
luxurious lifestyle could be maintained.

00:11:55.823 --> 00:11:58.613
Visitors described a house overflowing

00:11:58.613 --> 00:12:01.463
with portraits of Nelson, flags from his

00:12:01.463 --> 00:12:04.173
battles, and souvenirs of his victories.

00:12:04.683 --> 00:12:06.453
Some found it touching,

00:12:07.033 --> 00:12:08.893
others found it absurd.

00:12:09.533 --> 00:12:11.903
Nelson, by all accounts, loved it.

00:12:12.753 --> 00:12:16.543
Then, piece by piece, it fell apart.

00:12:17.453 --> 00:12:20.443
In April 1803, Sir William

00:12:20.443 --> 00:12:22.803
died, in Emma's arms, with her

00:12:22.803 --> 00:12:25.363
lover, Nelson, holding his hand.

00:12:26.423 --> 00:12:27.733
Whatever you make of their

00:12:28.163 --> 00:12:31.113
unusual three-sided household, it

00:12:31.113 --> 00:12:33.233
had ended with real tenderness.

00:12:34.723 --> 00:12:36.833
And assuming that Nelson would be

00:12:36.833 --> 00:12:39.583
able to support her, Sir William left

00:12:39.613 --> 00:12:42.233
Emma only a slice of his will: an

00:12:42.233 --> 00:12:44.463
income of eight hundred pounds a year.

00:12:45.253 --> 00:12:47.743
That is the equivalent of around fifty

00:12:47.743 --> 00:12:49.623
thousand pounds a year in today's

00:12:49.623 --> 00:12:52.223
money: enough for a comfortable life,

00:12:52.233 --> 00:12:54.843
of course, but Emma was used to the

00:12:54.843 --> 00:12:57.693
life of an ambassador's wife, a life

00:12:57.873 --> 00:13:00.663
of considerably more luxury than these

00:13:00.713 --> 00:13:02.553
eight hundred pounds could support.

00:13:03.773 --> 00:13:06.243
A few weeks later, war with France

00:13:06.253 --> 00:13:09.153
resumed, and Nelson went back to sea.

00:13:09.933 --> 00:13:12.123
He would spend the best part of the next

00:13:12.123 --> 00:13:14.683
two years at sea, keeping the French

00:13:14.693 --> 00:13:17.153
fleet trapped in its ports, while Emma

00:13:17.163 --> 00:13:19.463
waited at home with their young daughter.

00:13:20.655 --> 00:13:23.355
During those years, Emma gave birth to

00:13:23.355 --> 00:13:25.755
a second child by Nelson, another girl.

00:13:26.455 --> 00:13:28.815
The baby lived only a few weeks.

00:13:29.225 --> 00:13:32.105
Emma grieved almost entirely alone,

00:13:32.305 --> 00:13:33.995
keeping the news out of the papers

00:13:34.215 --> 00:13:36.025
and away from Nelson's family.

00:13:37.215 --> 00:13:40.465
In August 1805, he came home, and the

00:13:40.465 --> 00:13:42.965
family had twenty-five days together.

00:13:44.085 --> 00:13:47.025
Then the call came: the French and

00:13:47.025 --> 00:13:49.525
Spanish fleets were gathering at Cadiz.

00:13:50.515 --> 00:13:52.715
Nelson left Merton for the

00:13:52.725 --> 00:13:54.725
last time in the early hours

00:13:54.765 --> 00:13:57.665
of the 13th of September, 1805.

00:13:58.305 --> 00:14:00.265
He stopped at the door, and

00:14:00.305 --> 00:14:02.125
prayed over the bed of his

00:14:02.125 --> 00:14:03.685
sleeping four-year-old daughter.

00:14:05.115 --> 00:14:07.595
Five weeks later, on the morning of

00:14:07.615 --> 00:14:10.605
Trafalgar, he sat in his cabin and wrote

00:14:10.635 --> 00:14:12.645
the codicil we heard at the start of

00:14:12.745 --> 00:14:15.375
the episode, leaving Emma and Horatia

00:14:15.695 --> 00:14:18.305
as a legacy to his King and Country.

00:14:19.305 --> 00:14:21.505
The story of that day was the one

00:14:21.505 --> 00:14:23.555
you heard in part one, which ended

00:14:23.695 --> 00:14:25.645
with a French marksman spotting

00:14:25.645 --> 00:14:27.755
Nelson on the deck and killing him.

00:14:29.035 --> 00:14:30.862
The news reached Merton

00:14:31.185 --> 00:14:33.015
on the 6th of November.

00:14:33.825 --> 00:14:37.995
Emma gave one scream, fell back, and for

00:14:37.995 --> 00:14:40.915
ten hours could neither speak nor cry.

00:14:42.249 --> 00:14:44.449
And I'm sorry to say that if you are

00:14:44.459 --> 00:14:46.919
hoping the story becomes kinder to

00:14:46.919 --> 00:14:49.819
Emma from this point on, it does not.

00:14:50.982 --> 00:14:52.762
Britain gave Nelson the

00:14:52.802 --> 00:14:55.032
grandest funeral in its history.

00:14:55.682 --> 00:14:57.542
Thousands of people lined the

00:14:57.542 --> 00:14:59.352
streets of London as his coffin

00:14:59.362 --> 00:15:01.192
was carried to St Paul's Cathedral.

00:15:02.302 --> 00:15:04.412
Emma was not invited.

00:15:05.272 --> 00:15:07.702
The invitations went to the men of

00:15:07.702 --> 00:15:10.372
Nelson's family; the woman he had asked

00:15:10.372 --> 00:15:12.532
his country to care for, with his dying

00:15:12.532 --> 00:15:15.292
breath, was not welcome at his funeral.

00:15:16.652 --> 00:15:19.052
She spent the day at home with

00:15:19.052 --> 00:15:20.812
the women of his family, while

00:15:20.832 --> 00:15:22.932
Britain buried the man she loved.

00:15:24.165 --> 00:15:26.685
Then came the will, and the

00:15:26.685 --> 00:15:28.755
government's response to the codicil.

00:15:29.965 --> 00:15:30.915
Nelson's brother,

00:15:31.265 --> 00:15:32.695
William, was a clergyman.

00:15:33.625 --> 00:15:36.245
He was made an earl, and Parliament

00:15:36.275 --> 00:15:37.985
voted to give him an enormous

00:15:37.985 --> 00:15:39.885
sum of money to buy an estate,

00:15:40.085 --> 00:15:42.445
and a generous pension for life.

00:15:43.005 --> 00:15:44.755
Nelson's sisters received

00:15:44.775 --> 00:15:46.145
large payments too.

00:15:47.575 --> 00:15:50.595
Emma only received what Nelson had

00:15:50.595 --> 00:15:52.685
been able to leave her: the house at

00:15:52.685 --> 00:15:54.955
Merton, a lump sum of two thousand

00:15:54.955 --> 00:15:58.132
pounds,  and 500 pounds a year from his

00:15:58.132 --> 00:16:00.682
estate in Sicily, money that his brother

00:16:00.682 --> 00:16:03.392
controlled and refused to hand over.

00:16:04.672 --> 00:16:07.532
From the government, from the King and

00:16:07.582 --> 00:16:09.922
Country to whom Nelson had left her as

00:16:09.922 --> 00:16:12.672
a legacy, she received nothing at all.

00:16:13.442 --> 00:16:14.132
No pension.

00:16:14.672 --> 00:16:15.392
No provision.

00:16:15.762 --> 00:16:16.162
Nothing.

00:16:17.352 --> 00:16:19.672
The men who ran Britain were happy

00:16:19.672 --> 00:16:21.312
to celebrate Nelson the hero.

00:16:21.832 --> 00:16:23.502
They were not prepared to reward

00:16:23.502 --> 00:16:25.992
his mistress, a low-born woman of

00:16:25.992 --> 00:16:28.462
scandalous reputation, whatever the

00:16:28.462 --> 00:16:30.572
great man's dying wishes might have been.

00:16:31.472 --> 00:16:33.922
Her services at Syracuse, the

00:16:34.102 --> 00:16:36.742
services Nelson himself had put

00:16:36.752 --> 00:16:39.152
in writing, were simply forgotten.

00:16:40.545 --> 00:16:42.475
Now, it has to be said that

00:16:42.505 --> 00:16:44.535
Emma did not help herself.

00:16:45.085 --> 00:16:47.695
She had learned extravagance in the

00:16:47.705 --> 00:16:49.995
palaces of Naples, and this wasn't

00:16:50.035 --> 00:16:52.265
something she could easily unlearn.

00:16:52.845 --> 00:16:55.415
She kept entertaining, kept spending,

00:16:55.675 --> 00:16:57.645
kept giving money away to relatives

00:16:57.835 --> 00:17:00.165
and hangers-on, people who attached

00:17:00.165 --> 00:17:02.675
themselves to her for what they could get.

00:17:03.875 --> 00:17:06.745
The income that might have supported

00:17:06.775 --> 00:17:09.175
a careful widow could not begin to

00:17:09.175 --> 00:17:11.545
support the lifestyle of Lady Hamilton.

00:17:12.962 --> 00:17:16.382
Within a few years she was deep in debt.

00:17:17.102 --> 00:17:19.482
Merton, her house, was sold.

00:17:19.902 --> 00:17:20.812
Her mother died.

00:17:21.332 --> 00:17:23.902
One by one, Nelson's friends

00:17:24.172 --> 00:17:25.792
stopped answering her letters.

00:17:26.912 --> 00:17:29.172
She petitioned the government again

00:17:29.172 --> 00:17:30.962
and again, reminding them of the

00:17:30.962 --> 00:17:33.162
codicil, reminding them of Syracuse.

00:17:34.212 --> 00:17:35.842
Polite refusals came back,

00:17:35.982 --> 00:17:37.772
when anything came back at all.

00:17:39.065 --> 00:17:42.355
By 1813, her debts had caught up

00:17:42.355 --> 00:17:44.525
with her, and she was confined

00:17:44.535 --> 00:17:46.295
within the area around King's

00:17:46.375 --> 00:17:47.965
Bench debtors' prison in London.

00:17:48.985 --> 00:17:51.115
She was not locked in a cell; she

00:17:51.115 --> 00:17:53.145
lived with Horatia in cheap rooms

00:17:53.145 --> 00:17:55.605
nearby, under a system that allowed

00:17:55.625 --> 00:17:57.975
debtors to pay for a little freedom.

00:17:58.675 --> 00:18:01.475
But she was, effectively, a

00:18:01.475 --> 00:18:04.025
prisoner, a debtor like any other.

00:18:05.255 --> 00:18:09.005
And then in 1814 came a final blow.

00:18:09.785 --> 00:18:11.215
Nelson's private letters

00:18:11.215 --> 00:18:12.575
to her were published.

00:18:13.005 --> 00:18:16.205
They were intimate and full of passion.

00:18:17.535 --> 00:18:19.485
She always insisted she had

00:18:19.485 --> 00:18:21.605
nothing to do with it, and quite

00:18:21.605 --> 00:18:23.585
possibly the letters were taken

00:18:23.585 --> 00:18:25.675
and sold by someone she trusted.

00:18:26.915 --> 00:18:28.775
Whoever was responsible,

00:18:29.085 --> 00:18:30.735
the public turned on her.

00:18:32.035 --> 00:18:33.905
Facing arrest for her remaining

00:18:33.905 --> 00:18:35.875
debts, she did the only thing left.

00:18:36.415 --> 00:18:40.345
In July 1814, she and thirteen-year-old

00:18:40.345 --> 00:18:43.865
Horatia slipped aboard a ship and fled to

00:18:43.955 --> 00:18:46.135
Calais, on the northern coast of France,

00:18:46.505 --> 00:18:48.465
with about fifty pounds to her name.

00:18:49.566 --> 00:18:51.896
She spent her last months in cheap

00:18:52.116 --> 00:18:55.306
rented rooms, ill, drinking heavily

00:18:55.416 --> 00:18:57.326
to dull the pain, and consuming

00:18:57.346 --> 00:19:00.086
increasing amounts of laudanum, which

00:19:00.096 --> 00:19:02.456
was a liquid form of opium that was

00:19:02.456 --> 00:19:04.596
the standard painkiller of the age.

00:19:05.506 --> 00:19:08.956
Horatia, then thirteen years old, nursed

00:19:08.956 --> 00:19:11.336
her mother, and pawned what little

00:19:11.336 --> 00:19:13.456
they had left when the money ran out.

00:19:14.696 --> 00:19:17.586
Emma died on the 15th of January,

00:19:17.836 --> 00:19:20.956
1815, at the age of forty-nine.

00:19:21.616 --> 00:19:24.116
The most painted woman of her generation,

00:19:24.366 --> 00:19:26.596
the friend of a queen, the love of

00:19:26.616 --> 00:19:29.056
Britain's greatest hero, was buried

00:19:29.426 --> 00:19:31.776
in a simple grave in a foreign town,

00:19:32.326 --> 00:19:34.536
paid for by a kind acquaintance.

00:19:35.346 --> 00:19:36.646
The exact location of

00:19:36.646 --> 00:19:38.616
the grave was later lost.

00:19:39.779 --> 00:19:42.299
As for Horatia, she made it back to

00:19:42.299 --> 00:19:44.529
England, was taken in by Nelson's

00:19:44.529 --> 00:19:46.839
relatives, and went on to live a

00:19:46.899 --> 00:19:50.189
long, quiet, respectable life as

00:19:50.189 --> 00:19:52.264
the wife of a country clergyman.

00:19:53.079 --> 00:19:55.459
She raised ten children, and lived

00:19:55.619 --> 00:19:57.539
until the ripe old age of eighty.

00:19:58.739 --> 00:20:00.819
All her life, she proudly

00:20:00.829 --> 00:20:03.249
acknowledged Nelson as her father.

00:20:04.129 --> 00:20:07.289
And all her life, to the very end, she

00:20:07.289 --> 00:20:09.849
refused to admit that Emma Hamilton,

00:20:10.169 --> 00:20:12.469
the woman who had raised her, hidden

00:20:12.469 --> 00:20:15.719
her, fled to France with her, and died

00:20:15.719 --> 00:20:17.739
in poverty beside her, was her mother.

00:20:18.838 --> 00:20:21.608
Emma had climbed, against every rule

00:20:21.608 --> 00:20:23.628
of her society, from a blacksmith's

00:20:23.698 --> 00:20:25.658
cottage to the palaces of Europe.

00:20:26.418 --> 00:20:28.698
That society took its revenge

00:20:28.918 --> 00:20:30.468
in the end, and it reached

00:20:30.468 --> 00:20:32.728
even into her daughter's heart.

00:20:34.568 --> 00:20:36.708
OK, then, that is it for today's

00:20:36.738 --> 00:20:38.568
episode, and with that comes the

00:20:38.578 --> 00:20:40.548
end of this three-part mini-series

00:20:40.798 --> 00:20:42.928
on Nelson and Lady Hamilton.

00:20:43.348 --> 00:20:45.148
This, believe it or not, was

00:20:45.158 --> 00:20:46.998
originally a single episode, but

00:20:46.998 --> 00:20:48.878
there was so much detail and it got

00:20:48.888 --> 00:20:51.168
so long that I split it into three.

00:20:51.568 --> 00:20:53.738
And even then, there was a lot

00:20:53.798 --> 00:20:55.338
of wonderful stuff I had to leave

00:20:55.338 --> 00:20:57.158
out, but perhaps it has whetted

00:20:57.158 --> 00:20:59.048
your appetite to discover more.

00:21:00.038 --> 00:21:01.878
You've been listening to English Learning

00:21:01.878 --> 00:21:04.548
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:21:04.968 --> 00:21:08.108
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:21:08.118 --> 00:21:10.318
I'll catch you in the next episode.

